< 1. Build the case main board (change C5 of design/mfg/assembly/process.md, here PM). Do this before anything else. Nothing is powered at this step: no cell, no USB cable, no meter in voltage mode (PM C5: "nothing is powered at this step"). (a) Stencil: stainless steel, laser cut, 0.10 mm thick, cut from fab/case_main/gerber/case_main-F_Paste.gbr, 265 openings, smallest area ratio 0.764 (PM P2 table, row "Case main board, top (F)"; PM line 92 for why 0.10 and not 0.125 mm). The bottom face has no opening and gets no stencil (PM P2 table, row "Case main board, bottom (B)"). (b) Paste: Kester NP505-HR, SAC305, Type 4, no-clean (PM P1.2), kept in a refrigerator at 0 to 10 C (PM P1.2, storage). Print the top face only with a squeegee of 80 to 90 durometer (PM P6). Hand printing: align the stencil by its openings on the lands under the 10 times magnifier (PM P7, last bullet). (c) Placing: put every part of the top face where fab/case_main/case_main_position.csv says (rows with Side "top"; export_fab.sh, Part e of this file), with the values of fab/case_main_parts.csv. The 16 test pads TP1 to TP16 are on the bottom face and are copper only (Round 2, "Length used"): nothing is placed there. The USB socket J1 goes on with the other parts. The module U1 has moisture class 3 (E1 p.47): keep it in its sealed bag until it is placed (my reading, the same rule PM P3 gives for U3 of the ring). (d) Reflow once, with the profile of PM P3: ramp to 150 C at less than 2.5 C/s; soak 150 to 200 C in 90 to 110 s, then 200 to 217 C; 60 to 75 s above 217 C; at most 35 s above 230 C; peak 240 C, accepted 235 to 245 C on a thermocouple taped beside the most massive part; cool at 6 C/s or slower, and do not move the board while the solder is liquid. The radio module U1 accepts ONE reflow only (E1 p.47: "Solder the module in a single reflow."; PM P3 table, row "Case main board" U1: margin 0 passes, AT THE LIMIT). So this board goes through the oven once, and never again: a part that needs rework is put right by hand (see (e)), never by a second pass. (e) Inspect every joint at 10 times (PM P6, row Magnifier). Accept a part only when it sits in place and flat, and no solder bridges two neighbouring pads (the accept rule PM gives, from design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md line 99: "no solder bridge between neighbouring contacts", parts "in place and not tilted"); look hardest at the 0.5 mm pitch of the charger U2 (Round 1, Part c, "inside the 0.5 mm pitch of U2"). A joint that fails is put right by hand with the iron setting of PM P4 (340 C, 50 W, at most 7 s; my reading, PM gives no rework setting for these parts). The full written rule for a good reflowed joint is not in PM (PM C14: "a standard the lead names"): open, for the lead. The joints under the module U1 and under the thermal pad of U2 cannot be seen at 10 times; (g) below is the only check the files support, and it does not prove them all (it lists what it does not prove). (f) Then, by hand, the four shell slots of the USB socket J1 (the four plated slots, all with pad name SH, Part a of this file): Kester 275 wire 0.5 mm, iron 340 C, 50 W, at most 7 s per joint (PM C5; PM P4 table, rows "Case cell leads W5, W6" and "Case thermistor TH1 leads", the setting of the same kind of joint), the tip face no wider than the pad (PM P4, general rule). Inspect these joints at 10 times as in (e). The residue stays on the board; it is never washed (PM P1.5). (g) First power-up test of the case main board, before any wire is soldered: what is connected, the order, then one line per test pad and per rail, then what each hidden joint group shows and what the test does not prove (the indented lines below this step). The case has no firmware, so nothing here needs it. Keys: CS = design/ecad/case/sch-notes.md, CN = design/ecad/case/netlist.csv, BQ = design/datasheets/case_bq25895.pdf, ES = design/datasheets/case_esp32s3_mini1.pdf, SL = design/sim/circuit/pass-lines.md, SN = design/sim/circuit/sim-notes.md.
